<CaseStudy
  title="The EoL Report Writer"
  tagline="Turning the EoL Fixer's output into the EoL sections of an EN 15804 background
    report: contract-driven tables, fact-ID citations, no invented numbers."
  description="How a Claude Code skill assembles verifier-facing EoL report sections from the EoL Fixer's output: contract-driven tables, fact-ID citations, no invented numbers."
>

  <h2 id="the-problem">The problem</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The model output existed, but the report was still hand-assembled: figures copied from spreadsheets by hand, phrasing re-typed per project.</li>
    <li>Every hand-copy is a chance for the report to drift from the model, and a <span class="term" data-term="Verifier">verifier</span>'s favourite question is "where does this number come from?"</li>
    <li>Prose that quietly recomputes (a sum here, a rounded percentage there) creates inconsistencies nobody notices until verification.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="the-constraints">The constraints</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>EoL sections are verifier-facing: nothing can be approximate, and every number must trace back to the run.</li>
    <li>No client history allowed: unlike a report's opening sections, EoL text runs on standard practice, geography, and approved wording only.</li>
    <li>The tables are not negotiable: the engine has already decided which tables exist, which rows and columns they have, and every number in them.</li>
    <li>The target is a living Word document that colleagues may have open at the same time.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="what-it-does">What it does</h2>
  <p>
    A Claude Code skill that reads the <span class="term" data-term="EoL">EoL</span> Fixer's report workbook and builds the
    report's EoL content around it: places each table verbatim, writes the
    surrounding narrative from approved snippets, and cites every number through a
    fact ID. The workbook is treated as a contract: code has already decided the
    tables, the skill's job is to place them and write around them, never to rebuild
    them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="features">Features</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Contract-driven input.</strong> The workbook's contents tab declares which tables exist and, for any table that is empty or excluded, says why. The skill reports the reason instead of quietly building the table some other way.</li>
    <li><strong>Verbatim placement.</strong> Tables go in with their own headers, units, row order, and column order. Nothing is merged, re-sorted, dropped, or recomputed.</li>
    <li><strong>Audit columns stay home.</strong> Internal trace columns (dataset UUIDs, review markers) are used to resolve or audit a row, and never reach the report.</li>
    <li><strong>Fact-ID citation.</strong> Every number in prose, captions, and footnotes comes from a named fact in the workbook. The skill never computes a number, not even an easy sum; derivations go through a script that works from named facts.</li>
    <li><strong>Traceable drafts.</strong> When the draft is shown to the modeller, it lists the fact IDs used, so every number can be traced back in seconds.</li>
    <li><strong>Version awareness.</strong> Understands Fixer outputs across versions and tells the modeller when a re-run would unlock better report content.</li>
    <li><strong>The hard tables included.</strong> <span class="term" data-term="Biogenic carbon">Biogenic carbon</span> content with its supporting narrative, the dataset register with justification and geography, <span class="term" data-term="Module D">module D</span>, and data-quality summaries.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="the-receipts">The receipts</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Built across a sustained engagement adding EoL, biogenic-carbon, and dataset-register content to a real <span class="term" data-term="EN 15804">EN 15804</span> background report; the patterns held across every table and section.</li>
    <li>It is the second half of the production line: the <a href="/work/eol-fixer">EoL Fixer</a> generates, the report writer assembles, and the <a href="/epds">published EPDs</a> carry the result.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="why-built">Why it's built this way</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The engine decides, the prose arranges.</strong> Anything calculable belongs to the tool; the skill handles placement, wording, and structure. That split is what makes the output auditable.</li>
    <li><strong>No historical examples, by design.</strong> EoL sections deliberately see no past reports: standard practice and geography only, so one client's phrasing never leaks into another client's report.</li>
    <li><strong>Traceability beats fluency.</strong> A beautifully written number with no source is a liability. Fact IDs make every figure checkable in seconds.</li>
    <li><strong>Human approves before anything ships.</strong> The skill drafts and shows its sources; the modeller commits.</li>
  </ul>

  <details class="tech-details">
    <summary>Technical details</summary>
    <ul>
      <li>A Claude Code skill: a SKILL.md playbook plus reference documents (biogenic carbon, dataset register, narrative placement, table renumbering) and small scripts.</li>
      <li>Reads the workbook with code (openpyxl/pandas), never by eye: the contents tab is the index, and some tabs are hidden by design.</li>
      <li>Numbers enter prose only through <code>facts</code> rows, found by fact ID; totals and shares are computed by a dedicated script from named facts, never by the model.</li>
      <li>Writes into a live, shared .docx that may be concurrently open in Word.</li>
      <li>Ships with the house report skeleton and rules for where each kind of EoL content belongs.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How the contract flow works</h3>
    <p>
      The Fixer's workbook is a contract: a contents tab declares every table, its
      status, and the reason for any gap. The skill walks the contract, places each
      included table verbatim, and writes narrative that cites facts by ID. When a
      table is empty or excluded, the skill says so and asks whether the report
      should state the module does not apply, instead of filling the gap itself.
    </p>
